        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Evidence exists that the therapeutic effectiveness of some drugs may change in space. The objective of this study was to determine whether acetaminophen, a drug used frequently for pain relief by astronauts in space, actually reaches its site of action at concentrations sufficient to elicit a therapeutic response. The absorption and distribution of acetaminophen were examined to monitor potential changes in drug pharmacokinetics during spaceflight. 
<P>
APPROACH:<BR> 
A noninvasive saliva sampling technique was used. Tablets of acetaminophen were administered orally to 1 or 2 crew members on each of 7 missions (total of 13 subjects) twice before and at least once during space flight (ideally on flight day 3 or 4). Using the Saliva Collection Kit, saliva samples were collected 10 times, from 0 to 8 hours, after each drug administration. The concentration of acetaminophen in the samples, determined by high-performance liquid chromatography, was used to calculate parameters for pharmacokinetic evaluation.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
Salivary acetaminophen concentration profiles during space flight were  different from those obtained on the ground. Changes were more pronounced in the absorption phase than in the elimination phase. The time needed to reach maximum concentration of acetaminophen increased during flight, indicating that absorption time was prolonged. Changes in the maximum acetaminophen concentration depended on mission length. Results of this study supported predictions that drug dynamics change during flight and suggested that these changes depend upon mission length and individual physiologic responses to space flight. The limited in-flight data accumulated for this study were insufficient to characterize the degree and magnitude of pharmacokinetic changes and the mechanisms underlying them. 
<p>
        </div>

